  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nDry weather is expected through tonight as a high pressure lingers \nover the region. Highs in the 80s and low in the 60s will be in \nplace for the week. Temperatures look to near or rise above 90 \ndegrees for highs by Sunday into the new week, with increasing \nhumidity. The LREF shows a 50-80% chance of apparent temperatures \nbeing greater than 95 degrees on Monday afternoon and 50-70% chance \non Tuesday afternoon across the entire forecast area. The CPC has \nboth the 6-10 and 8-14 day outlooks with 60-80% chance of above \nnormal temperatures, so the sweltering weather isn't over quite yet \nfor this summer. This is said with the caveat of if we get some \nwidespread thunderstorm activity next week, the heat indices \nmay not be able to achieve 95-100+ degrees. \n\nA more active pattern will return late tonight as a cold front \npushes into the state by tomorrow. There is some discrepancies in \nthe CAMs with the timing of the earlier round of showers and \nthunderstorms. The HRRR has the activity entering the western CWA by \n10z, whereas the NAMNest has it entering by 15z. It looks to be that \nthere will be multiple rounds of showers and storms from Wednesday \nmorning to Thursday afternoon. MUCAPE values (and similarly SBCAPE \nvalues) are still looking to be around 1500-2500 J/kg and bulk shear \nvalues of 30-40 knots. SRH values look to be around 200-300 in the \nvicinity of the activity. The SPC has a marginal risk (level 1 of 5) \nin place for Wednesday west and northwest of a St Louis to Watseka \nline. If any severe weather occurs, it looks like conditions are \nbest from mid afternoon into the early evening Wednesday. All \nhazards are in play, with the main concern being damaging wind \ngusts. Overnight, the severe threat should wane without the daytime \nheating effect. WPC does have a slight risk ERO in place for nearly \nthe same area as the SPC outlook. The CAMs are hinting at some \ntraining over the same areas on Wednesday into Thursday. We will \nneed to monitor for a flash flooding threat.\n\nThe front is appearing more likely to linger over the area beyond \nWednesday, keeping us in a more active pattern through late week. \nThursday has a very similar environmental look in place, especially \nover west/southwestern IL, in the afternoon. Each day, from Thursday \nto Monday, could see some low risks for severe weather based off a \ncoarse look at thermodynamics in place and a look at the machine \nlearning models. We will continue to monitor the showery and severe \nthunderstorm possibilities beyond Thursday morning as we move \nforward in the week.",
